WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.930
Narration production begins with writing.

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:04.125
A good script is clear, conversational,

00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:07.419
and aligned with the screen or visual it supports.

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:11.078
It should explain relationships, guide attention, and provide useful context.

00:00:11.078 --> 00:00:15.103
It should not simply read a dense slide word for word.

00:00:15.103 --> 00:00:18.763
When narration and on-screen text duplicate each other too closely,

00:00:18.763 --> 00:00:21.690
the experience can feel redundant and mentally crowded.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.251
Recording voiceover requires a practical production mindset.

00:00:24.251 --> 00:00:28.643
Use a quiet space, consistent microphone placement, and a stable speaking pace.

00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:31.204
Record in manageable sections, label files clearly,

00:00:31.204 --> 00:00:34.863
and keep the script version connected to the audio version.

00:00:34.863 --> 00:00:36.327
Re-recording is sometimes necessary,

00:00:36.327 --> 00:00:39.621
but careful script review before recording prevents avoidable churn.

00:00:39.621 --> 00:00:43.646
Audio cleanup can include trimming, reducing background noise, evening out levels,

00:00:43.646 --> 00:00:45.476
and improving consistency across modules.

00:00:45.476 --> 00:00:50.233
Tools such as Audition or similar audio editors can support these tasks conceptually,

00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:52.428
but the goal is not overproduction.

00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:56.088
Learners usually need clean, understandable audio more than dramatic processing.

00:00:56.088 --> 00:00:59.015
Too much processing can sound unnatural or distracting.

00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:01.577
Captions and transcripts are essential production assets.

00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:06.700
Captions support learners who cannot hear the audio, learners in shared or noisy spaces,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.627
and learners who process information better with text.

00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:12.189
Transcripts help review, searchability, accessibility, and maintenance.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:16.580
Caption timing should be checked enough that the text supports the audio

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.409
rather than competing with it.

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:21.337
Keep narration assets organized and easy to revise.

00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:24.996
Use module-based file names, preserve approved scripts, track caption files,

00:01:24.996 --> 00:01:27.924
and avoid mixing source recordings with final exports.

00:01:27.924 --> 00:01:32.681
Course maintenance becomes far easier when the team can find the exact script,

00:01:32.681 --> 00:01:36.340
audio file, and caption file that correspond to a module.
